# WHY EVERY DESCRIPTION LINE IS INDENTED TWO SPACES. debconf reflows a
# description to the terminal width, so on a wide terminal the question
# arrives as 160-column paragraphs. A line with a second leading space is
# taken verbatim, and the dialog is then only as wide as its longest line.
# So: one sentence per line, broken at a clause when a sentence is longer
# than 70 characters, and nothing past column 72 -- that is what still fits
# an 80-column terminal without debconf breaking the line again.

Template: kerbridge-config/realm
Type: string
_Description: Kerberos realm for this deployment:
  KerBridge serves one Kerberos realm.
  Give it in upper case, for example EXAMPLE.SITE.
  KerBridge derives other values from it: the domain root,
  the DNS domain, the NetBIOS name, the short name of the
  domain controller, and two organizational units.
 .
  You cannot correct the realm later.
  It goes into the Samba database,
  and the permissions of every file carry an identifier from it.
  This is binary data in many places, and no edit can change it.
  To use a different realm, provision the domain again.
 .
  KerBridge writes the initial config files
  on the first installation only.
  If you leave this empty, it writes nothing.

Template: kerbridge-config/realm_invalid
Type: error
_Description: Invalid Kerberos realm
  A realm is a domain name with dots and no spaces,
  for example EXAMPLE.SITE.
  Your value is not a realm name.

Template: kerbridge-config/ldap_url
Type: string
_Description: LDAPS URL of the domain controller:
  KerBridge binds to this address.
  It accepts ldaps:// only, because plain LDAP and StartTLS
  send the bind password and each password write in clear text.
 .
  The proposed address uses a domain controller with the name
  "kerbridge" in the realm above.
  Change the host name if yours is different.

Template: kerbridge-config/ldap_url_invalid
Type: error
_Description: Invalid LDAPS URL
  The address must start with ldaps://.
  KerBridge refuses plain ldap:// and StartTLS.
  An address in that form fails later.

Template: kerbridge-config/tenant_id
Type: string
_Description: Cloud IdP tenant id:
  The Microsoft Entra ID tenant whose users this deployment admits.
  Give the tenant id as a UUID.
 .
  Leave this empty only for a host that does no synchronization
  and serves no sign-ins, for example an administrator machine
  with kerbridge-manage.
  KerBridge then writes the config files
  with no identity provider in them.
 .
  This is not a secret.

Template: kerbridge-config/broker_api_client_id
Type: string
_Description: UUID of the broker's Entra app, usually named "KerBridge broker API":

Template: kerbridge-config/public_client_id
Type: string
_Description: UUID of the client's Entra app, usually named "KerBridge public client":

Template: kerbridge-config/sync_client_id
Type: string
_Description: UUID of the sync Entra app, usually named "KerBridge sync":

Template: kerbridge-config/admission_group
Type: string
Default: KerBridge Allowed On-prem Users
_Description: Name of the cloud group whose members are admitted:
  Membership of this group admits an identity.
  KerBridge does not mirror the other users into the directory,
  and refuses each sign-in for them.
  Nothing works until a group with this name exists in the Entra tenant.
 .
  Any group is possible; this name is only a suggestion.
  When the realm is live, replace the name with the object id
  of the group in /etc/kerbridge/idp_entra.toml,
  so that a rename cannot point it elsewhere.
